<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Package xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
    Phase B5 (FINAL) of the "Property Package -> Portfolio Deal" rename program.

    STATUS: PREPARED, NOT AUTHORISED. Do not deploy without the user's explicit
    go-ahead. This file only DESCRIBES a deletion; it performs nothing on its own.
    Nothing in Phase B5 has been deployed to any org.

    WHAT THIS RETIRES: the legacy Property_Package__c object, the three lookups to
    it that live on OTHER objects, and the record page bound to it. Phases B1/B2
    created the Portfolio_Deal__c twin (object + 6 fields, live in usman-dpeg),
    B3 migrated the data, B4 renamed the Apex/LWC layer (deployed 2026-08-18,
    DeployRequest 0Afiw000000LpYzCAK, 36/36 components, 2077/2077 tests). B5 is
    the last step: nothing functional points at the old object any more.

    WHY THE 3 LOOKUPS ARE LISTED EXPLICITLY AND SEPARATELY FROM THE OBJECT:
    Lead.Property_Package__c, Opportunity.Property_Package__c and
    Property__c.Property_Package__c are CustomFields whose PARENT object is Lead /
    Opportunity / Property__c, not Property_Package__c. Deleting a CustomObject
    only cascades that object's OWN fields. These three are named here so their
    removal is explicit and reviewable rather than implied.

    ORDERING WITHIN THIS FILE: CustomField first, then Flexipage, then
    CustomObject. The dependents are listed before the thing they depend on, so
    that reading the file top-to-bottom matches the intended teardown order.
    The SetNull hazard the design doc warned about ("deleting the object first
    nulls the old lookups via SetNull and destroys the mapping") is MOOT here:
    B3's data migration completed and was verified (all Lead backfills correct,
    zero remaining un-migrated rows), so the old lookup values no longer need to
    survive this deploy. Ordering is now a clarity concern, not a data-safety one.

    KNOWN RESIDUAL RISK, AND ITS EXACT REMEDY (read before the real deploy):
    if the platform tears the 3 lookups down as part of the CustomObject deletion
    BEFORE it reaches this file's explicit CustomField members, those members
    resolve to nothing and the deploy emits
      problem: "No CustomField named: Lead.Property_Package__c found"
      problemType: "Warning"
    Because `ignoreWarnings` defaults to false and `rollbackOnError` is true, a
    Warning of that shape FAILS THE WHOLE ATOMIC TRANSACTION -- measured on this
    exact org during Phase B4 (see
    .claude/agent-memory-local/salesforce-admin/idempotent-post-destructive-redeploy-fails-safely.md).
    This is precisely what the standing validation-only dry-run is for: it will
    surface that warning with zero side effects. If it appears, the fix is to drop
    the three CustomField members and let the CustomObject member carry them, NOT
    to reach for --ignore-warnings, which would also mask real problems.

    NOT LISTED HERE, DELIBERATELY -- the old Layout
    (Property_Package__c-Property Package Layout):
    it belongs to Property_Package__c and goes with it. It is the ONLY component
    the org reports as depending on the object's fields: a live
    MetadataComponentDependency query on 2026-08-18 against all 9 field Ids
    (6 own fields + 3 lookups) returned exactly 9 rows, every one of them
    "Layout / Property Package Layout". Listing it explicitly would create the
    same "not found" Warning trap described above with no compensating benefit.

    ALSO NOT LISTED: any Profile. 41 profiles still carry stale fieldPermissions
    for previously-deleted fields (see manifest/portfolio-deal-a3/), and this org
    does not follow field deletion with a profile cleanup pass. profiles/** is
    .forceignore'd here, so those entries are neither deployed nor reconciled.
    Consistent with established practice, not a new gap.

    SWEEP EVIDENCE (fresh for B5, 2026-08-18, not inherited from B4's brief):
      - Repo: a comment-stripped scan of force-app + scripts found ZERO live
        (non-comment) references to Property_Package__c outside the components
        being deleted here. The four permission sets that granted it were cleaned
        in this same wave's package.xml. Remaining mentions are comment/doc text,
        one historical Apex test METHOD NAME
        (EmailToLeadServiceTest.createLeadFromExtracted_noPropertyPackageId_leavesTheLookupBlank,
        cosmetic, no object reference, left alone), and scripts/migrate-portfolio-deal-b3.apex
        (the already-executed B3 migration script, a historical record).
      - Org: MetadataComponentDependency returned 0 rows for the OBJECT and 9 rows
        for the 9 fields, all of them the doomed Layout (above). The 0-row object
        result is trustworthy because the same query shape returned rows for the
        fields in the same session -- an empty result here is a real absence, not
        a misfiring query.
      - Org: zero ListViews on Property_Package__c. No CustomTab exists for either
        the old or the new object.

    Deploy (only once authorised by the user; devops runs a validation-only
    dry-run first per standing project practice):
      sf project deploy start \
        --manifest manifest/portfolio-deal-b5/package.xml \
        --post-destructive-changes manifest/portfolio-deal-b5/destructiveChangesPost.xml \
        --target-org usman-dpeg \
        --test-level RunLocalTests --wait 30

    POST-destructive (not pre-): the constructive half must land FIRST. The
    Acquisition app's actionOverride is repointed off Property_Package_Record_Page,
    and four permission sets drop their Property_Package__c grants, in
    package.xml. Deleting PRE would leave the app and the permission sets naming
    components that no longer exist mid-transaction, which fails the whole deploy.

    Do NOT pass --purge-on-delete -- documented as non-functional on production
    orgs and fails silently rather than erroring (see manifest/portfolio-deal-a3).

    Rollback: a deleted custom object and its custom fields are recoverable from
    the org's recycle bin for 15 days, and all metadata is recoverable from git
    history. The FlexiPage is NOT soft-deleted by the platform; it is recoverable
    only by redeploying it from git, and only while Property_Package__c still
    exists to bind it to. Record data in Property_Package__c is superseded by the
    B3 migration into Portfolio_Deal__c and is not needed for rollback.
    -->
    <types>
        <members>Lead.Property_Package__c</members>
        <members>Opportunity.Property_Package__c</members>
        <members>Property__c.Property_Package__c</members>
        <name>CustomField</name>
    </types>
    <types>
        <members>Property_Package_Record_Page</members>
        <name>Flexipage</name>
    </types>
    <types>
        <members>Property_Package__c</members>
        <name>CustomObject</name>
    </types>
    <version>67.0</version>
</Package>
